ly achieves artificial general intelligence — human-level or beyond across all domains — then the employment landscape could change fundamentally. That's a real uncertainty. But we're not there yet, and most experts believe we're decades away, if it's even possible. In the meantime, we have a window to build the institutions, policies, and norms that can ensure the transition is managed well. And even in a future with AGI, the question would shift from "will there be jobs?" to "how do we distribute the benefits of abundance?" — which is a different, and potentially more hopeful, conversation. So I'm not saying the comparison to the printing press is perfect. It's not. But I am saying that AI's cognitive capabilities, while unprecedented, are still far from the full range of human intelligence. And that gap — between narrow AI and general human capability — is exactly where new, valuable, and durable employment opportunities will continue to emerge.
